# Code Standards

Professional quality, no shortcuts. These apply to all code, whoever writes it.

## TypeScript

- Strict mode is on and stays on. No `any`, no `@ts-ignore`, no `as` casts to silence errors. Use `unknown` + narrowing for untrusted data.
- Infer types where obvious; annotate public function signatures and API boundaries.

## Validation and errors

- Validate all external input (request bodies, query params, third-party API responses) with Zod at the boundary. Never pass unvalidated data to Prisma or business logic.
- No swallowed errors. Every `catch` either handles meaningfully or rethrows with context.
- API errors: correct status codes (400 validation, 404 missing, 500 unexpected) with a JSON body `{ "error": string }`.

## Testing

- Every feature ships with tests. Backend: Vitest + Supertest against the app. Frontend: Vitest + React Testing Library, testing behavior not implementation details.
- Tests must be able to fail: assert on real behavior, not just "doesn't throw".
- No `.only`, no skipped tests committed, never bypass the pre-commit hook.

## Style

- Prettier formats, ESLint lints — both must pass before commit.
- No commented-out code, no dead code. No TODO without a linked plan.
- Comments explain *why*, never narrate *what* the code does.

## Git

- Small commits, imperative mood: `feat(entries): add pagination to list endpoint`. Explain why in the body when non-obvious.
- Conventional prefixes: feat, fix, refactor, test, chore, docs.
